\title{Shifted Triangular Values and Square Spectra\\in the Displayed Anti-Fibonacci Sequence}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 16, 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We analyze the integer sequence displayed as $1,1,2,4,7,11,16,\ldots$ in a proposed anti-Fibonacci construction. The literal greedy description ``choose the smallest positive integer unequal to the sum of the preceding two terms'' does not generate these data. We isolate the coherent object forced by the displayed values: $A(0)=1$ and $A(n+1)=A(n)+n$. Its exact formula is
\[
A(n)=1+\frac{n(n-1)}2.
\]
Thus its quadratic leading coefficient is $1/2$, not $1/4$. We establish the consecutive-sum spectrum $A(n)+A(n+1)=n^2+2$, the individual-value spectrum $8A(n)-7=(2n-1)^2$, linear and arbitrarily large gaps, an exact density-zero counting law, unbounded error from every quarter-square approximation, and odd-index Fibonacci domination for all $n\ge6$. We also present direct algorithms for evaluation and spectral membership. The resulting theory replaces an inconsistent avoidance narrative with exact shifted-triangular and Diophantine structure.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The Fibonacci numbers $F_0=0$, $F_1=1$, and $F_{k+2}=F_{k+1}+F_k$ are the archetype of additive recurrence. A proposed ``anti-Fibonacci'' sequence was displayed as
\[
1,1,2,4,7,11,16,\ldots
\]
and described as choosing the smallest positive integer unequal to the sum of the preceding two terms. The rule and data are incompatible: excluding one positive integer generally leaves $1$ as the smallest choice. Consequently, the displayed sequence must be defined independently of that greedy description.

Its differences are $0,1,2,3,4,5,\ldots$. They determine a shifted triangular sequence, not an evasive recurrence. This correction changes the asymptotic coefficient and neighboring-ratio behavior, while yielding exact square spectra. We distinguish throughout between the values of this sequence, its consecutive sums, sums of arbitrary earlier terms, and the unrelated literal greedy construction.

\section{Definition and closed form}
\begin{definition}[Displayed anti-Fibonacci sequence]
For $n\in\mathbb N$, let
\[
A(0)=1,\qquad A(n+1)=A(n)+n.
\]
\end{definition}
This produces $A(0),\ldots,A(6)=1,1,2,4,7,11,16$.

\begin{lemma}[Exact first differences]
For every $n\ge0$,
\[
A(n+1)-A(n)=n.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
This is the defining recurrence rearranged.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]
For every $n\ge0$,
\[
A(n)=1+\frac{n(n-1)}2.
\]
Equivalently, $2A(n)+n=n^2+2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Iteration gives
\[
A(n)=A(0)+\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}k=1+\frac{n(n-1)}2.
\]
The equivalent identity follows by multiplication and rearrangement. One may instead induct: adding $n$ changes $1+n(n-1)/2$ into $1+n(n+1)/2$.
\end{proof}

Thus $A(n)=1+\binom n2$: it counts unordered pairs from an $n$-element set together with one distinguished extra object.

\begin{corollary}[Asymptotic growth]
\[
A(n)=\frac12n^2-\frac12n+1,
\qquad
\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{A(n)}{n^2}=\frac12.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Divide the closed form by $n^2$ to obtain $1/2-1/(2n)+1/n^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Neighboring ratios]
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{A(n+1)}{A(n)}=1.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The ratio is
\[
\frac{n^2+n+2}{n^2-n+2},
\]
which tends to $1$. In particular, it does not oscillate between $1$ and $2$.
\end{proof}

\section{Two exact square spectra}
\begin{theorem}[Shifted-Square Consecutive-Sum Theorem]
For every $n\ge0$,
\[
A(n)+A(n+1)=n^2+2.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Using the closed form at consecutive indices,
\[
A(n)+A(n+1)
=2+\frac{n(n-1)+n(n+1)}2=n^2+2.
\]
Geometrically, two consecutive triangular arrays form an $n$-by-$n$ square; the two unit shifts remain.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact consecutive-sum spectrum]
For a nonnegative integer $m$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $m=A(n)+A(n+1)$ for some $n\ge0$;
\item $m=n^2+2$ for some $n\ge0$;
\item $m-2$ is a perfect square.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first two assertions are equivalent by the preceding theorem, using the same index in both directions. The third is a restatement of the second.
\end{proof}

The consecutive sums are therefore $2,3,6,11,18,27,38,\ldots$.

\begin{theorem}[Odd-Square Value Criterion]
For every $n\ge0$,
\[
8A(n)-7=(2n-1)^2.
\]
A positive integer $m$ is a sequence value if and only if $8m-7$ is an odd square. The value $1$ occurs at indices $0$ and $1$; every later value occurs at a unique positive index.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitution gives
\[
8A(n)-7=8\left(1+\frac{n(n-1)}2\right)-7
=4n^2-4n+1=(2n-1)^2.
\]
Conversely, if $8m-7=q^2$ with $q$ odd, replace $q$ by $|q|$ and write $q=2n-1$ for $n\ge1$. Rearrangement yields $m=1+n(n-1)/2=A(n)$. Positive gaps for $n\ge1$ give uniqueness after the initial repetition.
\end{proof}

These independent spectra put individual values and consecutive sums on affine conics. Their intersections lead to quadratic Diophantine equations of Pell type, although no complete classification is asserted here.

\section{Gaps, counting, and density}
\begin{theorem}[Arbitrarily large gaps]
For every $C\ge0$, there is an $n\ge0$ such that
\[
A(n+1)-A(n)>C.
\]
Indeed, $n=C+1$ suffices when $C$ is integral.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The gap equals $n$.
\end{proof}

Absolute gaps diverge while neighboring ratios tend to $1$: their relative size is asymptotic to $2/n$.

\begin{theorem}[Density zero]
Let $N(X)$ be the number of distinct sequence values at most $X$. Then
\[
\lim_{X\to\infty}\frac{N(X)}X=0.
\]
Thus the value set has natural density zero.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $n\ge2$, the closed form gives $A(n)\ge n^2/4$. Hence $A(n)\le X$ implies $n\le2\sqrt X$, and therefore $N(X)\le2\sqrt X+1$. Dividing by $X$ and taking the limit proves the claim. The repeated initial value can only reduce the count. A sharp floor formula and its exact initial correction form a natural refinement.
\end{proof}

This density statement concerns only the value set of $A$. It does not classify all sums of earlier values or the separate greedy process.

\section{Failure of quarter-square growth}
\begin{theorem}[Unbounded quarter-square discrepancy]
For every constant $C\ge0$, there exists $n\ge0$ such that
\[
A(n)>\frac{n^2}{4}+C.
\]
For integral $C$, the explicit choice $n=4C+4$ suffices.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The exact discrepancy is
\[
A(n)-\frac{n^2}{4}=\frac{n^2}{4}-\frac n2+1,
\]
which tends to positive infinity. Direct substitution verifies the stated integral witness.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
There is no constant $K$ such that
\[
\left|A(n)-\left\lfloor\frac{n^2}{4}\right\rfloor\right|\le K
\]
for all $n$. Thus $A(n)\ne\lfloor n^2/4\rfloor+O(1)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Such a bound would imply $A(n)\le n^2/4+K+1$, contradicting the theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Millionth-index evaluation]
\[
A(1{,}000{,}000)=499{,}999{,}500{,}001,
\qquad
\frac{A(1{,}000{,}000)}{10^{12}}=0.499999500001.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $n=1{,}000{,}000$ into the closed form and divide by $10^{12}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Comparison with Fibonacci growth}
\begin{theorem}[Odd-index Fibonacci domination]
For every integer $n\ge6$,
\[
A(n)<F_{2n+1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At $n=6$, $A(6)=16<233=F_{13}$. Suppose $A(k)<F_{2k+1}$ for $k\ge6$. The recurrence gives $A(k+1)=A(k)+k$. Elementary induction on Fibonacci numbers gives $k\le F_{2k+2}$ in this range. Therefore
\[
A(k+1)<F_{2k+1}+F_{2k+2}=F_{2k+3},
\]
which completes the induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Odd-Fibonacci row-sum domination]
If a combinatorial triangular array has $n$th row sum $F_{2n+1}$, then its row sum strictly exceeds $A(n)$ for every $n\ge6$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Substitute the row-sum identity into the preceding theorem. This includes the standard Pascal--Riordan array realizing odd-index Fibonacci numbers.
\end{proof}

The result is an explicit comparison between shifted triangular accumulation and exponential recurrence growth.

\section{Algorithms and numerical diagnostics}
\begin{algorithm}[Direct evaluation]
Given $n\ge0$, return $1+n(n-1)/2$. This takes a constant number of arithmetic operations and constant auxiliary storage. With arbitrary-precision integers, the operands have $O(\log n)$ bits.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Value-spectrum membership]
Given an integer $m$, reject if $m<1$. Set $D=8m-7$ and $r=\lfloor\sqrt D\rfloor$. Accept exactly when $r^2=D$ and $r$ is odd; then $n=(r+1)/2$ is a positive index. For $m=1$, index $0$ is also valid. The algorithm uses one integer square root and constant auxiliary space.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Consecutive-sum membership]
Given $m$, reject if $m<2$. Set $r=\lfloor\sqrt{m-2}\rfloor$. Accept exactly when $r^2=m-2$; the unique index is then $n=r$.
\end{algorithm}

A useful diagnostic reports $A(n)/n^2$, $A(n)-n^2/4$, and $A(n+1)/A(n)$. At $n=10,100,1000$, the normalized values are respectively $0.46$, $0.4951$, and $0.499501$. Meanwhile the quarter-square discrepancy grows quadratically. Implementations should check redundantly that
\[
A(n+1)-A(n)=n,\quad
A(n)+A(n+1)=n^2+2,\quad
8A(n)-7=(2n-1)^2.
\]
All identities use exact integer arithmetic; floating point is needed only for presentation.

\section{Applications and limitations}
The first application is methodological: displayed data, verbal rules, and asymptotic conjectures are separate claims and must be tested against each other. A difference table detects the inconsistency immediately.

Computationally, direct formulas replace linear generation with constant-operation evaluation, while square criteria provide exact membership certificates. Combinatorially, $A(n)=1+\binom n2$ counts complete-graph edges plus one object, and two consecutive triangular parts combine into a square. Diophantinely, both spectra are affine conics, inviting congruence and Pell-equation methods. The Fibonacci comparison demonstrates, with an explicit threshold, the eventual separation of polynomial and exponential counting mechanisms.

The adjective ``anti-Fibonacci'' remains evocative but is not a literal description of the recurrence. The corrected sequence does not avoid preceding sums in the proposed greedy sense, and its ratios do not oscillate. The value spectrum, consecutive-sum spectrum, and arbitrary earlier-term sums are distinct objects; only the first two are classified here.

\section{Future work}
Five directions are immediate. First, classify intersections between the transformed odd-square value spectrum and the shifted-square consecutive-sum spectrum; the resulting affine conic should admit a Pell-type analysis. Second, derive sharp uniform discrepancy estimates for $N(X)-\sqrt{2X}$. Third, study families
\[
B(0)=b,\qquad B(n+1)=B(n)+an
\]
with $a>0$, including their spectra and exceptional Fibonacci coincidences. Fourth, determine the least Fibonacci-domination threshold and parameterized thresholds for general nonnegative quadratics. Fifth, classify the modular distribution of values using the criterion that $8m-7$ be an odd square; prime-power analysis and the Chinese remainder theorem should give exact local frequencies.

\section{Conclusion}
The displayed sequence is the shifted triangular sequence
\[
A(n)=1+\frac{n(n-1)}2.
\]
Its leading coefficient is $1/2$, neighboring ratios tend to $1$, gaps equal $n$, and quarter-square error is unbounded. Its values have density zero and admit the odd-square test $8A(n)-7=(2n-1)^2$. Its consecutive sums are exactly $n^2+2$, and from index $6$ onward it is dominated by $F_{2n+1}$. The apparent avoidance problem therefore resolves into a rigid and computationally transparent theory of triangular numbers, squares, and quadratic arithmetic.
\end{document}

